WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:14.145 --> 00:00:17.071
Stereotypes and
word choices often go hand in hand.

00:00:17.071 --> 00:00:21.935
For example, a recent study of massive
text datasets found that in the early

00:00:21.935 --> 00:00:26.801
1900s, Asians in the US were
characterized with words like barbaric or

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:32.500
monstrous, but today words like passive,
insensitive are more common.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.110
We see this play out in
the workplace as well.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:39.320
Job ads for professional roles
are often peppered with stereotypically

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.560
masculine words as are performance
reviews and letters of recommendation.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.650
These patterns have consequences.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.530
We know that when people are described
in ways that reflect stereotypes,

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:54.801
stereotypes are not only reinforced,
but they can also create disadvantages.

00:00:54.801 --> 00:01:01.301
[MUSIC]

00:01:01.301 --> 00:01:05.898
As an educator in a business school, I
wanted to know if the case studies we use

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:09.160
in our classes also reflect stereotypes.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.360
So a team of undergraduate research
assistants and I read the 250 unique case

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.990
studies taught in
the GSB MBA core curriculum.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.871
The goal was to analyze
the text of each case study,

00:01:21.871 --> 00:01:26.293
by scrutinizing the descriptions of
the protagonists, their situations and

00:01:26.293 --> 00:01:30.849
their cultural context, to look for
any potentially stereotypical language or

00:01:30.849 --> 00:01:32.067
language patterns.

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:37.211
[MUSIC]

00:01:37.211 --> 00:01:42.067
We discovered a number of ways in which
stereotype crept into the language that

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:44.680
used in this case studies.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:49.550
First, some case studies make sweeping
statements about the entire countries.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:54.069
For example, one case study
includes comments about the alleged

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:58.352
rigid hierarchy of Japan based on class,
age and education.

00:01:58.352 --> 00:02:02.450
Another case references how
physically attractive Brazilians are.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.540
Here rather than making sweeping
statements, examples and

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.510
data could instead be provided to support
the point being made in the case study.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:15.330
Similarly, some case studies fail
to provide sufficient context

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.510
around statements and
wind up just reinforcing this stereotypes.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.810
For example in a case study on
an erectile dysfunction drug,

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:28.990
there's a discussion about how ED leads
to a reduction in feeling masculine.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.085
And there's a reference to
a report in the case study,

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:36.950
that says that over 90% of men and
women reported that confidence in

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:41.970
a man's sexual ability is critical to
having a good loving relationship.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:46.070
Here the case study could have provided
a little bit more context, for example by

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:51.940
acknowledging that the study that they had
cited included only heterosexual couples.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.100
And while the comments about a reduction
and feeling masculine maybe directly

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.740
from the study that they cited, this type
of statement can reinforce the stereotype

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:05.650
than a man sexual ability is and should
be important to his sense of masculinity.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.330
Third, case study language can promote and
reinforce gender, racial and

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:12.900
ethnic stereotypes.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.420
For example, a case on a woman CEO opens
up with her reading to her three year old

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.040
son and kissing him goodnight before
joining her husband in the living room.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.899
Or another case quotes a CEO as
saying that the best lunches

00:03:25.899 --> 00:03:28.517
in Japan are prepared by wives at home.

00:03:28.517 --> 00:03:33.278
Most of these examples reinforce the
stereotype that women should be wives and

00:03:33.278 --> 00:03:35.019
mothers, but not leaders.

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:45.204
[MUSIC]

00:03:45.204 --> 00:03:49.271
First, we should train case writers,
about how easy it is for

00:03:49.271 --> 00:03:52.270
stereotypes to creep into case studies.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:56.590
So that they can be attune to the language
of leadership and strive to make sure that

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:02.140
the case studies they write do not
inadvertently reinforce stereotypes.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.560
We should also insist
that whenever possible,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.100
they include relevant context and
data when making sweeping claims.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.930
Here at the Stanford GSB
we're already doing this.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.417
We're working on training all of our
case writers on the best practices.

00:04:15.417 --> 00:04:21.496
[MUSIC]

00:04:21.496 --> 00:04:24.806
Second, for those of us using
case studies in the classroom,

00:04:24.806 --> 00:04:29.110
we should conduct an audit of language
used in all cases that we assign.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.860
This is something we're
also doing here at the GSB.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.860
We are currently writing cover memos for

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:37.470
any of the cases that we found with
potentially problematic language, and

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.270
we're making these memos
available to faculty.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:43.200
Well we obviously can't do this for
all of our cases.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.507
We believe that faculty,
once they're made aware of these issues,

00:04:46.507 --> 00:04:48.913
will begin to audit all of
the cases that they use.

00:04:48.913 --> 00:04:54.730
[MUSIC]

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:59.578
Third, as educators we can create a simple
rubric to help students see how they

00:04:59.578 --> 00:05:02.840
might identify stereotypes
in case studies.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:07.170
We can teach them to see if any of
the patterns we identified can be found

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.060
in any particular case they're reading.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.230
For example, we can ask them to
underline country description,

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:14.380
and descriptions of protagonists.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.770
And then evaluate these for
any sort of bias.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:22.250
Finally as educators it's important for
us to make this a teaching moment.

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:25.280
Once we have raised issues
of bias in the case studies,

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.180
we can facilitate a discussion
of how language impacts other

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:34.220
managerial situations, that all of our
students will experience in their futures.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:37.570
For example, bias can creep
into writing job descriptions,

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:42.050
letters of reference, hiring briefs or
performance evaluations.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:46.085
We should teach them to scrutinize
the language that they use as leaders.

00:05:46.085 --> 00:05:51.893
[MUSIC]

